          {
            "id": 598961,
            "name": "Matt Michels",
            "username": "mattmichels",
            "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/community.n8n.io/mattmichels/{size}/191164_2.png",
            "created_at": "2026-10-13T02:32:16.797Z",
            "cooked": "<p>Your plan to learn n8n by watching how someone else structures a real workflow, starting with webhooks and credentials, is a sound way to learn it.</p>\n<p>The closest thing I have is a self-hosted n8n workflow for a missed-call text-back. A signed Twilio-style voice webhook hits n8n, which verifies the X-Twilio-Signature, builds the text-back and sends it through a Twilio node. It then logs the miss to a Supabase table. Bad signatures get a 403, and Twilio errors go to a separate error-log branch. It’s test-built on localhost with SMS suppressed, so it isn’t in production.</p>\n<p>The same pattern shows up in a CSV import workflow I built: upload, allow-list the columns, validate, dedupe, then report inserted and skipped rows.</p>\n<p>Since you already test POST requests, I’d start in two steps:<br>\n(1) Move from ReqBin to signed requests, so you see why signature checks and replay protection matter.<br>\n(2) Add an error branch to one of your workflows, so failures get logged instead of vanishing.</p>\n<p>I can walk you through the exported workflow JSON on a live screen-share, node by node, and explain why each piece is where it is.</p>",
